#: source/store/file-system-object.md:1
msgid "File System Object"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object.md:3
msgid ""
"Nix uses a simplified model of the file system, which consists of file "
"system objects. Every file system object is one of the following:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object.md:6
msgid "\\[**Regular File**\\]{#regular}"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object.md:8
msgid "A possibly empty sequence of bytes for contents"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object.md:9
msgid ""
"A single boolean representing the [executable](https://en.m.wikipedia.org/"
"wiki/File-system_permissions#Permissions) permission"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object.md:11
msgid "\\[**Directory**\\]{#directory}"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object.md:13
msgid "Mapping of names to child file system objects"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object.md:15
msgid "\\[**Symbolic link**\\]{#symlink}"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object.md:17
msgid "An arbitrary string, known as the _target_ of the symlink."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object.md:19
msgid ""
"In general, Nix does not assign any semantics to symbolic links. Certain "
"operations however, may make additional assumptions and attempt to use the "
"target to find another file system object."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object.md:22
msgid ""
"See [the Wikipedia article on symbolic links](https://en.m.wikipedia.org/"
"wiki/Symbolic_link) for background information if you are unfamiliar with "
"this Unix concept."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object.md:24
msgid ""
"File system objects and their children form a tree. A bare file or symlink "
"can be a root file system object."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object.md:27
msgid ""
"Nix does not encode any other file system notions such as [hard links]"
"(https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hard_link), [permissions](https://"
"en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/File-system_permissions), timestamps, or other "
"metadata."
msgstr ""

